 bodies while standing, sitting, or lying down. Good posture involves training your body to stand, walk, sit, and lie in positions where the least strain is placed on supporting muscles and ligaments.\n\nKey Points:\n• Static vs Dynamic posture\n• Ideal alignment principles\n• Common postural deviations\n• Clinical significance of posture assessment\n\nThe Plumb Line:\nThe plumb line is an imaginary vertical line that serves as a reference for proper alignment. In ideal posture, when viewed from the side, this line passes through specific anatomical landmarks.`\n      }, {\n        id: 2,\n        title: 'Plumb Line Assessment Technique',\n        duration: '20 min',\n        type: 'lesson',\n        image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1571019614242-c5c5dee9f50b?w=600',\n        content: `Plumb Line Assessment\n\nThe plumb line should pass through these landmarks when viewed from the side:\n\nLateral View Landmarks:\n• Slightly anterior to lateral malleolus\n• Slightly anterior to axis of knee joint\n• Through greater trochanter\n• Through bodies of lumbar vertebrae\n• Through shoulder joint\n• Through bodies of cervical vertebrae\n• Through external auditory meatus\n\nAnterior/Posterior View:\nThe line should bisect the body into equal halves, passing through the nose, chin, sternum, umbilicus, and between the feet.`\n      }, {\n        id: 3,\n        title: 'Upper Crossed Syndrome',\n        duration: '25 min',\n        type: 'lesson',\n        image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1559757175-5700dde675bc?w=600',\n        content: `Upper Crossed Syndrome (UCS)\n\nUpper Crossed Syndrome is a muscle imbalance pattern in the head and shoulder region, first described by Dr. Vladimir Janda.\n\nTight/Overactive Muscles:\n• Upper trapezius\n• Levator scapulae\n• Pectoralis major & minor\n• Suboccipitals\n\nWeak/Inhibited Muscles:\n• Deep cervical flexors\n• Lower trapezius\n• Middle trapezius\n• Serratus anterior\n\nClinical Presentation:\nForward head posture, rounded shoulders, cervical hyperlordosis, and thoracic kyphosis.`\n      }, {\n        id: 4,\n        title: 'Lower Crossed Syndrome',\n        duration: '25 min',\n        type: 'lesson',\n        image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1571019613454-1cb2f99b2d8b?w=600',\n        content: `Lower Crossed Syndrome (LCS)\n\nLower Crossed Syndrome is a muscle imbalance pattern affecting the lumbar-pelvic-hip complex.\n\nTight/Overactive Muscles:\n• Hip flexors (iliopsoas, rectus femoris)\n• Erector spinae (lumbar portion)\n• Tensor fasciae latae\n\nWeak/Inhibited Muscles:\n• Gluteus maximus & medius\n• Abdominal muscles\n• Hamstrings (relatively)\n\nClinical Presentation:\nAnterior pelvic tilt, increased lumbar lordosis, hip flexion tendency, and protruding abdomen.`\n      }, {\n        id: 5,\n        title: 'Common Postural Deviations',\n        duration: '30 min',\n        type: 'lesson',\n        image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1576091160550-2173dba999ef?w=600',\n        content: `Postural Deviation Types\n\n1. Kyphosis-Lordosis Posture:\n• Increased thoracic kyphosis\n• Increased lumbar lordosis\n• Anterior pelvic tilt\n• Forward head position\n\n2. Flat-Back Posture:\n• Decreased lumbar lordosis\n• Posterior pelvic tilt\n• Hip and knee extension\n\n3. Sway-Back Posture:\n• Long kyphosis involving lumbar region\n• Posterior displacement of upper trunk\n• Anterior displacement of pelvis\n• Hip hyperextension\n\n4. Scoliosis:\nLateral curvature of the spine with rotational component.`\n      }, {\n        id: 6,\n        title: 'Clinical Applications',\n        duration: '20 min',\n        type: 'lesson',\n        image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551190822-a9333d879b1f?w=600',\n        content: `Clinical Applications of Posture Assessment\n\nTreatment Planning:\n• Identify muscles to strengthen\n• Identify muscles to stretch\n• Design corrective exercise programs\n• Monitor progress over time\n\nDocumentation:\nAlways document postural findings using standardized forms and photographs when possible.\n\nIntegration with Other Assessments:\n• ROM testing\n• Muscle length testing\n• Muscle strength testing\n• Functional movement screening`\n      }, {\n        id: 7,\n        title: 'Hands-on Practice Guide',\n        duration: '15 min',\n        type: 'lesson',\n        image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507034589631-9433cc6bc453?w=600',\n        content: `Practical Assessment Guide\n\nEquipment Needed:\n• Plumb line or posture grid\n• Goniometer (for angles)\n• Camera for documentation\n• Assessment forms\n\nAssessment Protocol:\n1. Patient in minimal clothing\n2. Relaxed standing position\n3. Assess from all views (anterior, lateral, posterior)\n4. Document deviations systematically\n5. Correlate findings with symptoms`\n      }, {\n        id: 8,\n        title: 'Quiz: Posture Fundamentals',\n        duration: '10 min',\n        type: 'quiz',\n        questions: 10\n      }]\n    },\n    'msk-screening': {\n      title: 'MSK Screening Masterclass',\n      description: 'Comprehensive guide to musculoskeletal screening tests including Y Balance, SLHB, and more.',\n      icon: 'bandage',\n      iconBg: '#E53935',\n      level: 'Intermediate',\n      duration: '4 hours',\n      lessons: [{\n        id: 1,\n        title: 'Introduction to MSK Screening',\n        duration: '20 min',\n        type: 'lesson',\n        image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1559757175-5700dde675bc?w=600',\n        content: `MSK Screening Overview\n\nLearn the fundamentals of musculoskeletal screening and why it's essential for injury prevention and rehabilitation.\n\nWhy Screen?\n• Identify risk factors before injury\n• Establish baseline measurements\n• Guide training modifications\n• Track rehabilitation progress\n\nKey Screening Tools:\n• Y-Balance Test\n• Single Leg Hop Battery\n• FMS\n• Joint-specific assessments`\n      }, {\n        id: 2,\n        title: 'Y-Balance Test Protocol',\n        duration: '30 